course: dictionary
lesson: 15539
title: Sing, sang, sung
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: «Sung» is a form of the verb «sing». The verb changes its shape: sing, sang, sung. Use «sung» after have, has, had, or was and were: «I have sung in the choir for two years.» «The anthem was sung at the start of the show.»
explain: Use «sang» on its own for something that happened in the past: «Yesterday I sang in the playground.» Do not write «I sung yesterday». Use «sung» only with a helper word like have, has, had or was.
explain: «Sung» can also describe something that is performed by singing. «The church held a sung service, with the prayers sung by the choir and the people.» Here «sung» tells you how the prayers were done, not that they were sung in the past.
fact: Sing, sang, sung follows the same pattern as ring, rang, rung, and drink, drank, drunk.
fact: «Sung» is the form to use after have, has, had, or was and were: «She has sung that song before.»
fact: «Sang» is the past tense on its own: «He sang all the way home.»
fact: As an adjective, «sung» means performed by singing rather than speaking, as in «sung prayers».
fact: An «unsung hero» is a hero whom nobody praises or thanks.
exercise: «The choir has ___ the same carol every year.» | sang | singing | *sung | sing
exercise: «Yesterday she sang. Today she has ___.» | sang | singed | sing | *sung
exercise: «The class joined in the sung prayers at the service.» What does sung mean here? | *Performed by singing, not speaking | Lost or forgotten | Written down on paper | Said quietly, in a whisper
test: «The school anthem was ___ by the whole school at assembly.» | sing | sang | *sung | singing
test: What does «unsung hero» mean? | *A hero whom nobody praises | A hero who won a prize for singing | A hero who can sing very well | A hero who is very old
exam: «The birds have ___ all morning long.» | sang | sing | sings | *sung
